      why: "<b>Kilo = 1000</b>: the same 'times ten' ladder as place value." },
    { q: "Which holds more: a cup or a bucket?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["a bucket","a cup","the same"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Which would take longer to fill?","A bucket is much bigger."],
      why: "<b>Capacity</b> is how much a container holds — measured in litres." },
    { q: "How many grams are in 2 kilograms?",
      type: "number", answer: 2000,
      hints: ["1 kg = 1000 g.","2 · 1000."],
      why: "<b>2 kg = 2000 g</b> — the times-1000 ladder again." },
    { q: "How many millilitres are in 1 litre?",
      type: "number", answer: 1000,
      hints: ["Milli means a thousandth.","Same as grams in a kilo."],
      why: "<b>1 litre = 1000 ml</b> — metric units love the number 1000." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "koerper", title: "Solid Shapes", klasse: 3, area: "geometrie",
  x: -412, y: 578, requires: ["formen"],
  teaser: "Flat shapes puff up into 3D: cubes, spheres and cylinders you can actually hold.",
  desc: "Naming everyday 3D solids and their flat faces.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A dice (die) is shaped like a …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["cube","sphere","cylinder","pyramid"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Six square faces.","All sides equal."],
      why: "A <b>cube</b> has 6 equal square faces — a 3D version of the square." },
    { q: "A ball is a …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["sphere","cube","cone","cuboid"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Perfectly round.","No corners at all."],
      why: "A <b>sphere</b> is round in every direction — no edges, no corners." },
    { q: "A tin of soup is shaped like a …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["cylinder","cube","sphere","pyramid"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Round with two flat ends.","Like a can."],
      why: "A <b>cylinder</b> has two circular ends and a curved side." },
    { q: "How many faces does a cube have?",
      type: "number", answer: 6,
      hints: ["Count the sides of a dice.","Top, bottom and four around."],
      why: "A cube has <b>6 square faces</b>." }
  ]
},

/* ================= GRADE 4 ================= */
{
  id: "schriftlich", title: "Written Multiplication", klasse: 4, area: "zahlen",
  x: 919, y: -195, requires: ["einmaleins", "zahlen1000"],
  teaser: "23 · 4 without counting a single apple – the columns do the work.",
  desc: "Big multiplications, split into small easy ones.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "23 · 4 = ?  (Trick: split it – 20 · 4 and 3 · 4.)",
      type: "number", answer: 92,
      hints: ["20 · 4 = 80.", "3 · 4 = 12. Now put them together."],
      why: "<b>Split into tens and ones</b>, multiply each part – this splitting idea returns in algebra as 'expanding'!" },
    { q: "105 · 3 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 315,
      hints: ["100 · 3 and 5 · 3.", "300 + 15."],
      why: "Zeros are easy passengers: multiply the parts, the places keep everything sorted." },
    { q: "Estimate before computing: 49 · 6 is close to …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["300 (because 50 · 6)", "240", "490", "54"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["49 is almost 50.", "50 · 6 = ?"],
      why: "<b>Estimate → compute → compare.</b> If your answer is far from the estimate, you caught your own mistake!" },
    { q: "A school has 4 floors with 128 students on each floor. How many students in total?",
      type: "number", answer: 512,
      hints: ["128 · 4: split it – 100·4, 20·4, 8·4.", "400 + 80 + 32."],
      why: "Big numbers bow to written methods – no pile of apples needed." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "geo4", title: "Right Angles & Grids", klasse: 4, area: "geometrie",
  x: -470, y: 814, requires: ["formen"],
  teaser: "Why are book corners, door corners and tile corners all the SAME corner?",
  desc: "The most important corner in the world – and paper with a built-in map.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A right angle looks exactly like …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["the corner of a sheet of paper", "a slanted roof", "a round curve", "half a circle"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Take any sheet of paper and look at a corner.", "Doors, books, windows – same corner everywhere."],
      why: "The <b>right angle</b> is geometry's favourite corner. Soon you will measure it: 90°." },
    { q: "Two streets that cross like a plus sign (+) are … to each other.",
      type: "choice", choices: ["perpendicular", "parallel", "the same street", "curved"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["They meet at a perfect corner.", "A paper corner fits exactly into the crossing."],
      why: "<b>Perpendicular</b> = meeting at a right angle. Builders check it every single day." },
    { q: "Train tracks that never ever meet are …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["parallel", "perpendicular", "crossing", "broken"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["They keep the same distance forever."],
      why: "<b>Parallel</b> = same distance everywhere. Perpendicular and parallel are the alphabet of construction." },
    { q: "On grid paper you walk 3 squares right, then 2 squares up. How many squares LEFT do you walk to stand exactly above your start?",
      type: "number", answer: 3,
      hints: ["Only the right-left steps matter for that.", "You went 3 right – so back …"],
      why: "Grid paper gives every place an <b>address</b> – you just planted the seed of coordinates!" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "bruch4", title: "Halves & Quarters", klasse: 4, area: "zahlen",
  x: 814, y: 470, requires: ["teilen"],
  teaser: "Half a pizza, a quarter of an hour – parts of things have names too.",
  desc: "The first fractions: when one whole thing gets fairly split.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "You cut an apple into 2 equal pieces. One piece is called …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["one half", "one quarter", "one third", "just a bit"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Two equal parts.", "You and a friend each get one …"],
      why: "A <b>half</b> = 1 of 2 equal parts. Equal is the magic word!" },
    { q: "Half of 10 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["Share 10 between 2.", "5 and 5."],
      why: "Half of an amount = sharing between 2 – fractions and division are cousins." },
    { q: "A quarter means …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["1 of 4 equal parts", "1 of 2 equal parts", "4 whole things", "the biggest part"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Quarter … like 4.", "Cut twice: first in half, then each half in half."],
      why: "<b>Quarter</b> = 1 of 4 equal parts. A quarter of an hour = 15 minutes – you use fractions daily!" },
    { q: "A quarter of 20 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["Share 20 among 4.", "20 : 4."],
      why: "Parts of amounts = <b>division in disguise</b>. In grade 5 the fraction adventure really begins!" }
  ]
},

{
  id: "schriftdiv", title: "Written Division", klasse: 4, area: "zahlen",
  x: 1041, y: 92, requires: ["schriftlich"],
  teaser: "Sharing big numbers on paper — the neat method that never runs out of fingers.",
  desc: "Dividing larger numbers step by step, with and without remainders.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "48 ÷ 6 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 8,
      hints: ["6 times what makes 48?","6 · 8 = 48."],
      why: "<b>Division</b> asks 'how many equal groups?' — multiplication run backwards." },
    { q: "Share 20 sweets equally among 4 children. Each child gets …",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["Split 20 into 4 equal piles.","20 ÷ 4."],
      why: "Fair sharing is division you can see." },
    { q: "72 ÷ 8 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 9,
      hints: ["8 times what is 72?","8 · 9."],
      why: "Division undoes multiplication — <b>8 · 9 = 72</b>." },
    { q: "100 ÷ 4 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 25,
      hints: ["Quarter of 100.","Four 25s make 100."],
      why: "Splitting 100 into 4 equal parts gives <b>25</b>." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "dezimal4", title: "First Decimals", klasse: 4, area: "zahlen",
  x: 907, y: 244, requires: ["bruch4"],
  teaser: "A dot that unlocks the in-between: numbers that live between the whole ones.",
  desc: "Meeting decimal points through halves and quarters of money and measures.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "One half written as a decimal is …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["0.5","0.1","5.0","1.5"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Half of one whole.","Like 50 cents of a euro."],
      why: "<b>0.5</b> means five tenths — decimals extend counting below 1." },
    { q: "Which is bigger: 0.5 or 0.2?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["0.5","0.2","they're equal"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Five tenths vs two tenths.","Think 50 cents vs 20 cents."],
      why: "Compare decimals digit by digit, just like whole numbers." },
    { q: "One quarter (¼) written as a decimal is …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["0.25","0.5","0.75","0.1"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Like 25 cents of a euro.","A quarter of 1.00."],
      why: "<b>¼ = 0.25</b> — money makes decimals intuitive." },
    { q: "0.7 + 0.3 = ? (write it as a whole number)",
      type: "number", answer: 1,
      hints: ["Seven tenths plus three tenths.","Ten tenths = one whole."],
      why: "Tenths add up to a <b>whole</b>: 0.7 + 0.3 = 1." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "flaeche4", title: "Area & Perimeter", klasse: 4, area: "geometrie",
  x: -670, y: 659, requires: ["geo4"],
  teaser: "Walk around the edge (perimeter) or cover the inside (area) — two ways to measure a shape.",
  desc: "Perimeter as the distance around, area as the squares inside.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A square has sides of 4 cm. The perimeter (all four sides added) is … cm",
      type: "number", answer: 16, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["Four equal sides.","4 + 4 + 4 + 4."],
      why: "<b>Perimeter</b> is the total distance around a shape." },
    { q: "A rectangle is 5 squares wide and 3 squares tall. How many squares cover it (its area)?",
      type: "number", answer: 15,
      hints: ["Rows times columns.","5 · 3."],
      why: "<b>Area</b> = length × width — counting the squares inside." },
    { q: "A rectangle 6 squares wide and 2 tall covers how many squares (its area)?",
      type: "number", answer: 12,
      hints: ["Rows times columns.","6 · 2."],
      why: "<b>Area = length × width</b>." },
    { q: "A square with side 5 cm has a perimeter of … cm",
      type: "number", answer: 20, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["Four equal sides.","5 · 4."],
      why: "<b>Perimeter</b> of a square = 4 × side." }
  ]
},

/* ================= GRADE 5 ================= */
{
  id: "zaehlen", title: "Numbers & Place Value", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 1175, y: -103, requires: ["zahlen1000"],
  teaser: "Why can we write ANY number, however gigantic, with just 10 digits?",
  desc: "The beginning of everything (α!): how our number system is built – and why it never ends.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Using the digits 3, 7, 0 and 5 (each exactly once), build the SMALLEST possible number.",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3057", "0357", "3075", "5730"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Small digits should stand as far to the left as possible.",
              "A number may not start with 0. So which digit comes first?",
              "Start with 3, then the 0, then ascending: 3 – 0 – 5 – 7."],
      why: "The value of a digit depends on its <b>place</b> – that is the place-value system." },
    { q: "How much is the 7 worth in the number 4726?",
      type: "number", answer: 700,
      hints: ["Count the places from the right: ones, tens, hundreds …",
              "The 7 sits in the hundreds place."],
      why: "Each place is worth <b>10 times</b> the one to its right." },
    { q: "Which number comes directly before 1 000 000 (one million)?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["999 999", "999 000", "1 000 001", "990 999"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Directly before means: exactly 1 smaller.", "Compute 1 000 000 − 1."],
      why: "Natural numbers <b>never end</b> – every one has a successor. Just like this web of knowledge." },
    { q: "Round 34 567 to the nearest thousand.",
      type: "number", answer: 35000,
      hints: ["Look at the hundreds digit: 5 or more rounds up.",
              "34 567 lies between 34 000 and 35 000. 567 is more than half of 1000."],
      why: "Rounding makes big numbers manageable – <b>estimating</b> lets you sanity-check any result in seconds." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "addsub", title: "Clever Adding & Subtracting", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 929, y: -728, requires: ["zaehlen"],
  teaser: "How did 9-year-old Gauss add 1+2+3+…+100 in seconds?",
  desc: "Don't grind – be smart: swap, bundle, adjust.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Compute cleverly in your head: 37 + 89 + 63 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 189,
      hints: ["You may swap the order. Which two numbers fit together nicely?",
              "37 + 63 = 100.", "100 + 89 = ?"],
      why: "Swapping and bundling makes mental math easy – order doesn't matter when adding." },
    { q: "701 − 398 = ?  (No written method needed!)",
      type: "number", answer: 303,
      hints: ["398 is almost 400.", "701 − 400 = 301 … but now you took away 2 too many.", "301 + 2 = ?"],
      why: "The <b>adjusting</b> trick: compute with a round number, then compensate." },
    { q: "The Gauss trick: 1+2+3+…+10. Pair numbers from the outside in (1+10, 2+9, …). What do you get?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["5 pairs, each summing to 11 → 55", "10 pairs summing to 10 → 100", "5 pairs summing to 10 → 50", "11 pairs summing to 5 → 55"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["1+10 = 11, 2+9 = 11, 3+8 = 11 … always 11!", "10 numbers form 5 such pairs."],
      why: "<b>Spotting patterns</b> beats brute-force arithmetic – that's exactly how young Gauss thought." },
    { q: "Anna has €250. She buys shoes for €89 and gets €15 from grandma. How much does she have now (€)?",
      type: "number", answer: 176,
      hints: ["First money leaves: 250 − 89.", "250 − 89 = 161. Then something comes in."],
      why: "Word problems mean <b>translating</b> everyday life into numbers. You'll practise that constantly here." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "multdiv", title: "Multiplying & Dividing", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 1150, y: -264, requires: ["addsub", "schriftlich"],
  teaser: "A sheet of paper folded 42 times would reach beyond the Moon. Multiplication explodes!",
  desc: "Multiplying is repeated adding – and dividing is its reverse.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Compute smartly: 4 · 17 · 25 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 1700,
      hints: ["You may swap factors too. Which two give a round number?",
              "4 · 25 = 100.", "100 · 17 = ?"],
      why: "Swap and bundle factors – multiplication is flexible." },
    { q: "A class of 24 students gets 168 stickers, shared fairly. How many per student?",
      type: "number", answer: 7,
      hints: ["We need 168 : 24.", "Ask it backwards: 24 · ? = 168. Try 24 · 7."],
      why: "Division is the <b>reverse</b> of multiplication – sharing fairly means dividing." },
    { q: "13 · 99 in your head – which route is smartest?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["13·100 − 13", "13·90 + 13", "10·99 + 99", "13·9·11"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["99 is almost 100.", "Take 13 hundreds, then give back the 13 you overpaid."],
      why: "<b>Distributing</b>: 13·(100−1) = 13·100 − 13 = 1287." },
    { q: "A bus holds 50 people. 214 students go on a school trip. How many buses are needed?",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["214 : 50 = 4 remainder 14.", "The remaining 14 students must come along too!"],
      why: "With remainders, the <b>context</b> decides whether to round up or down." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "teilbarkeit", title: "Divisibility & Primes", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 1213, y: 304, requires: ["multdiv"],
  teaser: "Why can you be SURE that 111 111 111 is divisible by 9 – without computing anything?",
  desc: "Secret rules reveal what's inside a number. And primes are its atoms.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Which of these numbers is divisible by 3?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["4527", "1234", "2026", "1000"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["There is a trick using the digits.",
              "Add up the digits. If THAT sum is divisible by 3, so is the number.",
              "4+5+2+7 = 18 – and 18 : 3 works."],
      why: "<b>Digit-sum rule</b>: 3 (and 9) divide a number exactly when they divide its digit sum." },
    { q: "A prime has exactly 2 divisors: 1 and itself. Which of these is prime?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["51", "57", "61", "63"], answer: 2,
      hints: ["Check digit sums – whatever 3 divides isn't prime.",
              "51: 5+1=6 ✓ divisible by 3. 57: 12 ✓. 63: 9 ✓.", "Only one is left."],
      why: "Primes are the <b>atoms of numbers</b> – every other number is built from them." },
    { q: "Break 60 into prime factors: 60 = 2 · 2 · 3 · ?",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["60 = 2 · 30 = 2 · 2 · 15.", "And 15 = 3 · ?"],
      why: "Every number has exactly <b>one</b> prime factorization – its fingerprint." },
    { q: "Is 2027 divisible by 2, by 5, or by 10?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["No – by none of them", "By 2", "By 5", "By 10"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["You only need the last digit!",
              "By 2: last digit even. By 5: last digit 0 or 5. By 10: last digit 0."],
      why: "<b>Last-digit rules</b>: the final digit reveals divisibility by 2, 5 and 10." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "brueche1", title: "Discovering Fractions", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 979, y: 711, requires: ["multdiv", "bruch4"],
  teaser: "Which is more: half of a small pizza or a quarter of a giant one? It depends!",
  desc: "Between the whole numbers there is infinite space – fractions make it visible.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A pizza is cut into 8 equal slices. You eat 3. What fraction of the pizza is that?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3/8", "8/3", "3/5", "1/3"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["The bottom (denominator) says into how many parts it was cut.", "8 parts in total, 3 of them are yours."],
      why: "A fraction is a <b>share</b>: the numerator (top) counts, the denominator (bottom) names the size of the parts." },
    { q: "Which fraction is exactly as big as 1/2?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3/6", "2/6", "3/4", "1/4"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Out of 6 pieces – how many make one half?", "3 of 6 is exactly half."],
      why: "<b>Expanding and simplifying</b> change only the notation, not the size: one value, many names." },
    { q: "Which is bigger: 2/3 or 3/4?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3/4", "2/3", "both equal", "impossible to compare"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Ask the other way round: how much is MISSING to a whole?",
              "2/3 misses 1/3; 3/4 misses 1/4. Where is less missing?",
              "1/4 is smaller than 1/3 – so 3/4 is closer to a whole."],
      why: "Compare cleverly: the <b>look-at-the-gap</b> trick is often faster than any calculation." },
    { q: "3/4 of 20 gummy bears is how many gummy bears?",
      type: "number", answer: 15,
      hints: ["First find 1/4: that's 20 : 4.", "1/4 = 5, so 3/4 = 3 · 5."],
      why: "'of' with fractions means: divide by the denominator, multiply by the numerator." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "dezimal", title: "Decimal Numbers", klasse: 5, area: "zahlen",
  x: 626, y: 1000, requires: ["brueche1"],
  teaser: "0.5 or 0.45 – which is bigger? More digits does NOT mean more value!",
  desc: "The place-value system continues to the right: tenths, hundredths, thousandths …",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Which number is the largest?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["0.5", "0.45", "0.405", "all equal"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Compare place by place after the decimal point.", "Write 0.5 as 0.50. Which is more: 50 or 45 hundredths?"],
      why: "After the point the place-value system continues: <b>tenths, hundredths, …</b> – place beats digit count." },
    { q: "Write 3/10 as a decimal.",
      type: "number", answer: 0.3,
      hints: ["Tenths live in the first place after the point.", "3 tenths = 0.3."],
      why: "Decimals ARE fractions – just with denominators 10, 100, 1000, …" },
    { q: "Write 1/4 as a decimal.",
      type: "number", answer: 0.25,
      hints: ["Expand 1/4 to hundredths: 1/4 = ?/100.", "25 hundredths."],
      why: "Fractions and decimals are <b>two languages for the same idea</b>. Now you can translate." },
    { q: "2.7 + 0.35 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 3.05,
      hints: ["Point under point! Write 2.70 + 0.35.", "70 + 35 hundredths = 105 hundredths = 1.05. And the 2?"],
      why: "When adding, <b>equal place values</b> meet – the decimal point keeps everything in line." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "geo-grund", title: "Points, Lines & Segments", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: -692, y: 956, requires: ["geo4"],
  teaser: "With just two ideas – point and line – you can build all of geometry. Like LEGO.",
  desc: "The basic building blocks of every shape. Small but mighty.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "How many different straight lines pass through TWO distinct points?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["exactly one", "two", "infinitely many", "none"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Imagine a string stretched between two nails.",
              "Can you stretch it a different way without bending it?"],
      why: "Through 2 points passes <b>exactly 1</b> line – one of geometry's basic laws." },
    { q: "What distinguishes a segment from a line?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["A segment has a start and an end, a line has neither", "A line is always drawn longer", "A segment is curved", "There is no difference"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Think of a pedestrian crossing versus a laser beam into space.", "Which of the two stops somewhere?"],
      why: "Segment: two ends. Ray: one end. Line: <b>no end</b> – infinite like this web." },
    { q: "Two lines in the plane that NEVER meet are called …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["parallel", "perpendicular", "identical", "skew"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Think of train tracks.", "They keep exactly the same distance everywhere."],
      why: "<b>Parallel</b> = same distance everywhere. <b>Perpendicular</b> = crossing at a right angle. Two key words of geometry." },
    { q: "What is the MAXIMUM number of intersection points of 3 lines?",
      type: "number", answer: 3,
      hints: ["Draw it! Each line can cross each other line at most once.",
              "Count the pairs: line 1&2, line 1&3, line 2&3."],
      why: "You just <b>counted possibilities</b> (combinatorics) – it hides inside simple drawings." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "winkel", title: "Angles", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: -1059, y: 586, requires: ["geo-grund"],
  teaser: "Why does a circle have exactly 360°? A 4000-year-old inheritance from Babylon.",
  desc: "Angles measure turning – from clock hands to skateboard tricks (a 360!).",
  exercises: [
    { q: "The minute hand moves from 12 to 3. Through which angle did it turn?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["90°", "30°", "180°", "120°"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["A full round (12 → 12) is 360°.", "12 to 3 is exactly a quarter of the round."],
      why: "An angle is a <b>turn</b>. Full turn 360°, quarter turn 90° = right angle." },
    { q: "An angle measures 47°. What is it called?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["acute", "obtuse", "right", "straight"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Compare it with 90°.", "Everything below 90° is called …?"],
      why: "Acute < 90° = right < obtuse < 180° = straight. <b>Names create order.</b>" },
    { q: "Two angles sit next to each other on a straight line. One measures 135°. How big is the other?",
      type: "number", answer: 45, unit: "°",
      hints: ["A straight line is a straight angle: 180°.", "180 − 135 = ?"],
      why: "<b>Adjacent angles</b> on a line add up to 180° – find angles without measuring: detective work!" },
    { q: "How big is the angle between the clock hands at exactly 1 o'clock?",
      type: "number", answer: 30, unit: "°",
      hints: ["The 360° are shared between 12 hour steps.", "360 : 12 = ?"],
      why: "Angles are everywhere – even inside your clock. (At 3 o'clock? Right: 90°.)" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "symmetrie", title: "Symmetry", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: -1168, y: 165, requires: ["geo-grund"],
  teaser: "Why do butterflies, castles and the letter A look so 'right'?",
  desc: "Mirroring without anything changing – the secret order behind beauty.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Which capital letter has a vertical mirror line?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["A", "F", "G", "J"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Imagine a mirror standing in the middle of the letter.",
              "Fold the left half onto the right: where does it match exactly?"],
      why: "<b>Line symmetry</b>: mirroring leaves the figure unchanged." },
    { q: "How many lines of symmetry does a square have?",
      type: "number", answer: 4,
      hints: ["Horizontal and vertical through the centre – that's two already.", "Don't forget the two diagonals!"],
      why: "The more regular the figure, the more symmetries. The square manages 4 lines." },
    { q: "Point P lies 3 cm to the LEFT of the mirror line. Where does its image P' land?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["3 cm to the right of the line", "3 cm to the left", "exactly on the line", "6 cm to the right"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["A mirror preserves distance – only the side changes.", "Same distance, other side: 3 cm again."],
      why: "Reflection = <b>same perpendicular distance on the other side</b>. That's how you construct mirror images exactly." },
    { q: "Which figure has INFINITELY many lines of symmetry?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["circle", "square", "equilateral triangle", "rectangle"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["For which figure can you put the mirror through the centre in ANY direction?"],
      why: "The circle is the most symmetric figure of all – that's why nature uses it constantly." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "rechteck", title: "Rectangle & Square", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: 169, y: 1209, requires: ["geo-grund", "multdiv"],
  teaser: "Fence or lawn – perimeter or area? Knowing the difference saves real money.",
  desc: "The two most important questions about any shape: how long is the edge? How much fits inside?",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A rectangle is 7 m long and 4 m wide. What is its perimeter (m)?",
      type: "number", answer: 22, unit: "m",
      hints: ["Perimeter = walking once around.", "7 + 4 + 7 + 4 = ?"],
      why: "P = 2·(l+w). The perimeter is the <b>edge length</b> – for fences, frames, borders." },
    { q: "The same rectangle (7 m × 4 m): what is its area (m²)?",
      type: "number", answer: 28, unit: "m²",
      hints: ["Area = how many 1-m² tiles fit inside.", "7 rows of 4 tiles – or simply 7 · 4."],
      why: "A = l·w. Area is the <b>content</b>, measured in squares. Multiplying replaces counting!" },
    { q: "Two rectangles both have perimeter 20 m. Do they automatically have the same area?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["Not necessarily – try 9×1 and 5×5!", "Yes, always", "Only if one is a square", "Area is always perimeter times 2"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["9×1 and 5×5 both have perimeter 20. Compute both areas.", "9·1 = 9 m², but 5·5 = 25 m²!"],
      why: "Same perimeter ≠ same area! The <b>square</b> squeezes the most area out of a given perimeter." },
    { q: "A square has perimeter 36 cm. How long is one side (cm)?",
      type: "number", answer: 9, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["A square has 4 equal sides.", "36 : 4 = ?"],
      why: "<b>Thinking backwards</b>: from the result to the starting value. That's how researchers think." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "quader", title: "Cuboid & Cube", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: 579, y: 1186, requires: ["rechteck"],
  teaser: "How much air is in your room? After this topic you can work it out.",
  desc: "From flat area into space: the third dimension.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A cube has edge length 3 cm. What is its volume (cm³)?",
      type: "number", answer: 27, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["Volume = how many 1-cm³ mini-cubes fit inside.",
              "3 layers of 3·3 mini-cubes each.", "3 · 3 · 3 = ?"],
      why: "V = a·a·a = a³. That's why 'to the power of 3' is called <b>cubed</b>!" },
    { q: "A shoebox measures 30 cm · 20 cm · 10 cm. Volume (cm³)?",
      type: "number", answer: 6000, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["V = length · width · height.", "30 · 20 = 600, then · 10."],
      why: "V = l·w·h – one floor layer of area, stacked up in height." },
    { q: "How many faces, edges and corners does a cuboid have?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["6 faces, 12 edges, 8 corners", "8 faces, 12 edges, 6 corners", "6 faces, 8 edges, 12 corners", "4 faces, 12 edges, 8 corners"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Grab a box and really count!", "Faces: floor + lid + 4 walls."],
      why: "Check: faces + corners − edges = 6+8−12 = 2. That holds for <b>every</b> such solid (Euler's formula) – a hidden law of nature!" },
    { q: "An aquarium (cuboid) is 50 cm long and 20 cm wide. It should hold 30 litres (1 L = 1000 cm³). How high must the water stand (cm)?",
      type: "number", answer: 30, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["30 litres = 30 000 cm³.", "V = l·w·h  →  30 000 = 50 · 20 · h.", "50·20 = 1000. So h = 30 000 : 1000."],
      why: "Using formulas <b>backwards</b> = solving equations. This tool will become huge later." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "koord", title: "Coordinate System", klasse: 5, area: "geometrie",
  x: -1083, y: -415, requires: ["geo-grund"],
  teaser: "'Row 7, seat 12' – cinemas, battleships and Google Maps all use the same idea.",
  desc: "Every point gets an address made of numbers. Geometry meets arithmetic.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "The point P(3|2): what do the two numbers mean?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["3 to the right, then 2 up", "3 up, then 2 to the right", "3 and 2 are added", "row 3, column 2 from the top"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["First comes x (horizontal), then y (vertical).", "Memory aid: x comes before y in the alphabet – walk first, then climb."],
      why: "Coordinates are a point's <b>address</b>: first x (right), then y (up)." },
    { q: "Which point lies exactly on the y-axis?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(0|4)", "(4|0)", "(4|4)", "(1|4)"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["On the y-axis you have walked 0 steps to the right.", "So x must be 0."],
      why: "The axes are zero-lines: y-axis ⇔ x = 0, x-axis ⇔ y = 0." },
    { q: "A(1|1) and B(5|1). How long is the segment AB?",
      type: "number", answer: 4,
      hints: ["Both points are equally high (y = 1). Count only the x-steps.", "5 − 1 = ?"],
      why: "Distances can be <b>calculated</b> instead of measured – geometry turns into numbers!" },
    { q: "A(1|1), B(5|1) and C(5|4) are three corners of a rectangle. Where is the fourth corner D?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(1|4)", "(4|1)", "(1|5)", "(5|5)"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["D lies directly above A – and at the same height as C.", "Take the x from A and the y from C."],
      why: "Figures become <b>pairs of numbers</b>. This bridge between geometry and algebra carries you to graduation – and beyond." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "groessen", title: "Units & Measures", klasse: 5, area: "groessen",
  x: -972, y: -786, requires: ["messen", "uhrzeit"],
  teaser: "In 1999 a Mars probe was destroyed because two teams used different units. Units are serious!",
  desc: "Metres, kilograms, seconds, euros – numbers with meaning.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "2.5 m = ? cm",
      type: "number", answer: 250, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["1 m = 100 cm.", "2.5 · 100 = ?"],
      why: "Converting often just means <b>shifting the decimal point</b> – our units are built in the base-10 system." },
    { q: "A movie lasts 135 minutes, which is 2 hours and ? minutes.",
      type: "number", answer: 15, unit: "min",
      hints: ["1 hour = 60 minutes.", "135 = 60 + 60 + ?"],
      why: "Time ticks in a <b>base-60 system</b> – an inheritance from Babylon, just like the 360°!" },
    { q: "Which is heavier: 1500 g or 1.4 kg?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["1500 g", "1.4 kg", "both equal", "impossible to compare"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Bring both to the same unit.", "1.4 kg = 1400 g. And now?"],
      why: "Comparing only works in the <b>same unit</b> – otherwise you're comparing apples with oranges." },
    { q: "1 m² = ? dm²",
      type: "number", answer: 100, unit: "dm²",
      hints: ["1 m = 10 dm. A square metre is a square with 10 dm sides.", "10 · 10 = ?"],
      why: "Area units jump in steps of <b>100</b>, because area has two directions. (Volume: steps of 1000!)" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "daten1", title: "Data & Charts", klasse: 5, area: "daten",
  x: -122, y: -1173, requires: ["zahlen100"],
  teaser: "A picture says more than 1000 numbers – if you can read it. And if it isn't lying!",
  desc: "Collect data, display it, see through it.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Class survey, favourite animal: dog 9, cat 7, hamster 4, snake 2. In the bar chart, the dog bar is …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["the tallest", "the shortest", "as tall as the cat bar", "twice as tall as the cat bar"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["The height of a bar shows the count.", "9 is the biggest number."],
      why: "Charts translate numbers into <b>pictures</b> – comparison at a glance." },
    { q: "Dog 9, cat 7, hamster 4, snake 2: how many children were surveyed in total?",
      type: "number", answer: 22,
      hints: ["Add up all the bars.", "9 + 7 + 4 + 2 = ?"],
      why: "The sum of all categories gives the <b>total count</b> of the data." },
    { q: "A chart's y-axis starts at 90 instead of 0. Bar A: 95, bar B: 100. What's the effect?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["B looks hugely bigger although the difference is tiny", "Completely neutral", "A disappears entirely", "The difference looks smaller"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Only the part above 90 is visible: so 5 vs. 10.", "10 looks twice as big as 5 – giant difference!"],
      why: "Charts can <b>trick you</b>. If you can read data, neither ads nor headlines can fool you." },
    { q: "Temperature curve: 12° at 6 am, 21° at noon. By how many degrees did it get warmer?",
      type: "number", answer: 9, unit: "°",
      hints: ["Difference = bigger value minus smaller value.", "21 − 12 = ?"],
      why: "Line charts show <b>change over time</b> – perfect for weather, fevers, stock prices." }
  ]
},

{
