      why: "Everything you've learned, united: decimals AND negatives on <b>one</b> number line." },
    { q: "(−2) · (−3) = ? And how do we know?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["6 – the pattern forces it: (−2)·3=−6, (−2)·2=−4, (−2)·1=−2 … each step +2", "−6, minus stays", "1", "−12"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Look at the sequence: (−2)·3 = −6, (−2)·2 = −4, (−2)·1 = −2, (−2)·0 = 0 …",
              "Each step grows by 2. How does it continue at (−2)·(−1)?"],
      why: "'Minus times minus is plus' is no dogma – <b>the pattern leaves no other choice</b>. Math explains itself." },
    { q: "(−3/4) · 8 = ?",
      type: "number", answer: -6,
      hints: ["First compute 3/4 of 8.", "That's 6 – and which sign?"],
      why: "Fraction rules and sign rules <b>interlock</b> – two topics, one toolbox." },
    { q: "The temperature falls from 4.5 °C by 7 °C. New temperature (°C)?",
      type: "number", answer: -2.5, unit: "°C",
      hints: ["4.5 − 7 = ?", "4.5 − 4.5 = 0, then 2.5 further down."],
      why: "Rational numbers describe the real world: temperatures, bank balances, depths below sea level." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "potenzen", title: "Powers", klasse: 7, area: "zahlen",
  x: 1535, y: -683, requires: ["multdiv"],
  teaser: "A lily pad doubles daily and covers the lake on day 30. When was the lake HALF covered?",
  desc: "Repeated multiplication – and why it wrecks every intuition.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "2⁵ = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 32,
      hints: ["2·2·2·2·2 – step by step: 2, 4, 8, …", "16 · 2 = ?"],
      why: "A power is shorthand for repeated multiplication: 2⁵ means <b>the factor 2, five times</b>." },
    { q: "The lake is FULLY covered on day 30; the lily area doubles daily. When was it half covered?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["day 29", "day 15", "day 10", "day 28"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Think BACKWARDS from the end.", "What happens from one day to the next? It doubles."],
      why: "<b>Exponential growth</b> ambushes intuition: almost to the very end, everything looks harmless." },
    { q: "10³ · 10² = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["10⁵", "10⁶", "100⁵", "10⁹"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Write it out: (10·10·10) · (10·10).", "How many tens stand there now?"],
      why: "aᵐ · aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ – the exponents <b>add</b>. You just discovered a law of powers yourself!" },
    { q: "3⁴ = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 81,
      hints: ["3 · 3 = 9. Then twice more times 3.", "9 · 3 = 27, and 27 · 3 = ?"],
      why: "'Squared' = area, 'cubed' = volume – powers carry geometry inside them." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "termeb", title: "Brackets & Binomial Formulas", klasse: 7, area: "algebra",
  x: 1079, y: -1287, requires: ["gleich1", "potenzen"],
  teaser: "(a+b)² = a²+b²? You wish! A square picture shows you the truth.",
  desc: "Rearranging terms like a pro – with pictures instead of memorizing.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "3(x + 4) = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3x + 12", "3x + 4", "x + 12", "7x"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["3 parcels, each containing: one x and one 4.", "So triple both."],
      why: "<b>Expanding</b>: the factor in front visits EVERYONE inside the bracket." },
    { q: "A square with side a+b is cut by two lines. Which pieces appear?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["One a² square, TWO a·b rectangles and one b² square", "Just a² and b²", "Four a·b rectangles", "2a and 2b"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Draw it: split each side into a and b, cut horizontally and vertically.",
              "Four fields appear – two of them are identical rectangles."],
      why: "(a+b)² = a² + 2ab + b² – the binomial formula is a <b>picture</b>, not a vocabulary item. The 2ab are the two rectangles!" },
    { q: "(x + 3)² = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["x² + 6x + 9", "x² + 9", "x² + 3x + 9", "2x + 6"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Use the picture: a² + 2ab + b² with a = x and b = 3.", "2 · x · 3 = 6x and 3² = 9."],
      why: "Understood once, used a thousand times – patterns are <b>reusable thinking</b>." },
    { q: "Mental math trick: 101² = (100+1)² = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 10201,
      hints: ["a² + 2ab + b²: so 100² + 2·100·1 + 1².", "10 000 + 200 + 1 = ?"],
      why: "The binomial formula as a <b>mental-math superpower</b>. Algebra is practical!" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "prozent2", title: "Percent & Interest", klasse: 7, area: "groessen",
  x: -1359, y: -987, requires: ["prozent1"],
  teaser: "3 % interest per year – after 2 years more than 6 % gain. How can that be?",
  desc: "Money that earns money: percentages become powerful.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "€500 sit in an account at 3 % yearly interest. How many € of interest after 1 year?",
      type: "number", answer: 15, unit: "€",
      hints: ["3 % of 500.", "1 % of 500 = €5. And 3 %?"],
      why: "Interest = percent of the capital. <b>Money that your money earns.</b>" },
    { q: "The €515 stay another year (again 3 %). Balance afterwards (€)?",
      type: "number", answer: 530.45, unit: "€",
      hints: ["Now it's 3 % of 515 – not of 500!", "3 % of 515 = 15.45. So 515 + 15.45."],
      why: "<b>Compound interest</b>: interest earns its own interest. Growth on growth – exponential, like the lily pads!" },
    { q: "A price rises by 10 %, then falls by 10 %. What's true at the end?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["The price is slightly LOWER than at the start", "Exactly the same as at the start", "Slightly higher", "Twice as high"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Try €100: +10 % → €110.", "Now −10 % OF €110 = €11. So 110 − 11 = ?"],
      why: "Percentages always refer to the <b>current</b> value – +10 % and −10 % do NOT cancel. (99 ≠ 100!)" },
    { q: "A phone drops from €240 to €180. What percentage discount is that?",
      type: "number", answer: 25, unit: "%",
      hints: ["Savings: €60. What share of 240 is that?", "60/240 = 1/4 = ?"],
      why: "Percentage = share · 100. Whoever can compute <b>backwards</b> sees through every ad." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "pythagoras", title: "Pythagorean Theorem", klasse: 7, area: "geometrie",
  x: -790, y: 1483, requires: ["flaeche2", "potenzen"],
  teaser: "A 2500-year-old theorem lives in every GPS, every blueprint and every screen diagonal.",
  desc: "The most famous theorem in mathematics: a² + b² = c².",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Right triangle with legs 3 and 4. The squares on the sides have areas 9, 16 and 25. What do you notice?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["9 + 16 = 25", "All three are equal", "25 − 16 = 11", "9 · 16 ≈ 25"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Add the two small square areas.", "9 + 16 = ?"],
      why: "<b>a² + b² = c²</b>: the two leg-squares together fill the hypotenuse-square exactly. Area proves it!" },
    { q: "Legs 6 and 8. How long is the hypotenuse?",
      type: "number", answer: 10,
      hints: ["c² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64.", "c² = 100. Which number times itself gives 100?"],
      why: "Two sides reveal the third – <b>measuring becomes computing</b>." },
    { q: "A 5 m ladder leans on a wall, its foot 3 m from the wall. How high does it reach (m)?",
      type: "number", answer: 4, unit: "m",
      hints: ["Wall, ground and ladder form a right triangle. Which side is the hypotenuse?",
              "The ladder itself! So h² = 5² − 3²."],
      why: "The theorem also runs <b>backwards</b>: leg² = hypotenuse² − other leg²." },
    { q: "Does a² + b² = c² hold in EVERY triangle?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["No – only if the angle opposite c is a right angle", "Yes, always", "Only in equilateral triangles", "Only if c is the shortest side"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Test an equilateral triangle with side 1: then 1 + 1 = 1. Does that fit?",
              "The right angle is the decisive ingredient."],
      why: "Theorems have <b>conditions</b>! And it flips: if a²+b²=c², the angle IS right – builders check corners with a 3-4-5 rope to this day." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "massstab", title: "Scale & Similarity", klasse: 7, area: "geometrie",
  x: -1510, y: 737, requires: ["schluss", "dreieck"],
  teaser: "How do you measure a pyramid's height using its shadow? Thales knew – 2600 years ago.",
  desc: "Same shape, different size – from maps to giants.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Map scale 1:50 000. Two towns are 4 cm apart on the map. How far in reality (km)?",
      type: "number", answer: 2, unit: "km",
      hints: ["1 cm on the map = 50 000 cm in reality.", "4 · 50 000 = 200 000 cm. How many km is that?"],
      why: "Scale = <b>ratio</b> map : reality. Your proportional reasoning + your unit skills, working together." },
    { q: "Model car 1:24. The real car is 4.8 m long. How long is the model (cm)?",
      type: "number", answer: 20, unit: "cm",
      hints: ["4.8 m = 480 cm.", "480 : 24 = ?"],
      why: "Divide by the scale = shrink. Multiply = back to reality." },
    { q: "Thales' trick: a 1 m stick casts a 1.5 m shadow. A tree casts 9 m at the same moment. Tree height (m)?",
      type: "number", answer: 6, unit: "m",
      hints: ["At the same moment, for EVERYTHING: shadow = 1.5 × height.", "So height = 9 : 1.5."],
      why: "<b>Similar triangles</b>: same shape → same ratios. Measuring without climbing – exactly how Thales measured the pyramids!" },
    { q: "A photo is enlarged to double length AND double width. Its area becomes …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["4 times as big", "twice as big", "3 times as big", "8 times as big"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Try it: 2 cm × 1 cm → 4 cm × 2 cm.", "Before 2 cm², after 8 cm² – so times ?"],
      why: "Lengths ×k → areas ×k² → volumes ×k³. <b>That's why giants can't exist</b>: weight would outgrow bone strength!" }
  ]
},
{
  id: "statistik", title: "Mean, Median & Co", klasse: 7, area: "daten",
  x: -118, y: -1676, requires: ["daten1"],
  teaser: "'1.4 children per family on average' – yet nobody has 1.4 children. What does the mean really say?",
  desc: "Key figures summarize data – and can mislead.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Your test scores: 2, 1, 3, 2 (grades). What is the mean?",
      type: "number", answer: 2,
      hints: ["Add everything, then divide by the count.", "(2+1+3+2) : 4 = ?"],
      why: "The mean <b>shares out fairly</b>: as if every test had gone equally well." },
    { q: "Salaries in a small firm: 2000, 2100, 2200, 2300 and 20 000 €. What describes the 'middle' more honestly?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["The median 2200 – the middle value of the sorted list", "The mean 5720", "The maximum", "The sum"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Compute the mean: (2000+2100+2200+2300+20000) : 5.",
              "€5720 – does anyone here 'typically' earn that?",
              "Sort the values and take the middle one."],
      why: "<b>Outliers</b> distort the mean – the median stays calm. Choose your tool consciously!" },
    { q: "What is the median of 3, 9, 5, 1, 7?",
      type: "number", answer: 5,
      hints: ["Sort first: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.", "The middle one of the five."],
      why: "Median = middle of the <b>sorted</b> data: half lie below, half above." },
    { q: "Your average after 4 tests is 2.0. What grade do you need on the 5th for an average of 1.8?",
      type: "number", answer: 1,
      hints: ["5 tests averaging 1.8 means a total of 5 · 1.8 = 9.", "So far you have 4 · 2 = 8. What's missing to 9?"],
      why: "Computing <b>backwards</b> with the mean – how athletes and students plan their goals." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "hebel", title: "Physics: Levers", klasse: 7, area: "physik",
  x: -987, y: -1359, requires: ["schluss"],
  teaser: "'Give me a place to stand and I shall move the Earth' – Archimedes meant this law.",
  desc: "Small force, long arm: inverse proportionality you can touch.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A seesaw balances when force × arm is equal on both sides. A 30 kg child sits 2 m from the pivot. Where must a 60 kg adult sit?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["1 m from the pivot", "2 m", "4 m", "0.5 m"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Child side: 30 · 2 = 60.", "Adult: 60 · ? = 60."],
      why: "The <b>law of the lever</b>: force × arm = force × arm. Double the weight, half the arm." },
    { q: "Balance: left 40 kg at 1.5 m. Right: 20 kg at ? m",
      type: "number", answer: 3, unit: "m",
      hints: ["Left: 40 · 1.5 = 60.", "20 · ? = 60."],
      why: "This is exactly your <b>inverse proportionality</b>: the product stays constant." },
    { q: "Bottle opener, wheelbarrow, crowbar – they all work because …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["long arm with small force beats short arm with big force", "metal is strong", "levers reduce weight", "friction helps"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Where do you push, where does the load sit – which arm is longer?",
              "You trade a long path for a small force."],
      why: "Levers <b>trade distance for force</b>. Nothing is free: you push less hard but move further." },
    { q: "You push with 50 units of force on a 60 cm crowbar arm; the load arm is 5 cm. Force at the tip?",
      type: "number", answer: 600,
      hints: ["50 · 60 = load force · 5.", "3000 : 5 = ?"],
      why: "12× the force! Now you know why a crowbar opens what fingers never could." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "trapez", title: "Trapezoid & Composite Areas", klasse: 7, area: "geometrie",
  x: -464, y: 1615, requires: ["flaeche2"],
  teaser: "No formula memorized? No problem – cut any shape into pieces you already know.",
  desc: "The art of decomposition: every straight shape surrenders.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A trapezoid has parallel sides a = 8 and c = 4 and height 3. Glue a flipped copy next to it – what appears?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["A parallelogram with base a+c = 12 and height 3", "A rectangle 8×3", "A triangle", "A bigger trapezoid"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Rotate the copy by 180° and attach it to the slanted side.",
              "The long side of one meets the short side of the other: total base 8+4."],
      why: "Two trapezoids = one parallelogram → A = <b>(a+c)/2 · h</b>. The same trick as with the triangle!" },
    { q: "So: a = 8, c = 4, h = 3. Trapezoid area = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 18,
      hints: ["(a+c)/2 · h.", "(8+4)/2 = 6, then · 3."],
      why: "(a+c)/2 is the <b>average</b> of the two parallel sides – a trapezoid is a rectangle with averaged width." },
    { q: "An L-shaped room: a 5 m × 4 m rectangle with a 2 m × 1 m corner missing. Floor area (m²)?",
      type: "number", answer: 18, unit: "m²",
      hints: ["Big rectangle minus the missing corner.", "20 − 2 = ?"],
      why: "<b>Decompose or subtract</b> – composite shapes are just known shapes in disguise." },
    { q: "Which strategy computes the area of ANY straight-edged shape?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["Split it into triangles and rectangles", "Only measuring helps", "One magic formula for all shapes", "Count the corners and multiply by a side"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["What shapes can you already compute?", "Every polygon can be cut into triangles."],
      why: "<b>Triangulation</b> – the same trick used by land surveyors and 3D computer graphics." }
  ]
},

{
