      why: "That's how a bike computer works: it counts turns and multiplies <b>by the circumference</b>." },
    { q: "Circle area: A = r²·π. Pizza with r = 10 cm (π ≈ 3.14): A = ? (cm²)",
      type: "number", answer: 314, tol: 1, unit: "cm²",
      hints: ["r² = 100.", "100 · 3.14 = ?"],
      why: "A = r²π – picture it: cut the circle into thin cake slices and lay them into an <b>almost-rectangle</b> (sides r and r·π)." },
    { q: "Small pizza: r = 10 cm. Large pizza: r = 20 cm. The large one has … as much area.",
      type: "choice", choices: ["4 times", "2 times", "3 times", "π times"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Area depends on r².", "20² = 400 versus 10² = 100."],
      why: "Double radius = <b>quadruple pizza</b>! The k² effect from similarity – better order one large than two small." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "zylinder", title: "Cylinders & Round Solids", klasse: 8, area: "geometrie",
  x: -497, y: 1855, requires: ["kreis", "prisma"],
  teaser: "Why are nearly all cans round? Answer: mathematics saves metal.",
  desc: "The prism idea meets the circle – and on the horizon waits the sphere.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A cylinder is a 'round prism'. Can: r = 5 cm, h = 10 cm, π ≈ 3.14. Base area (cm²)?",
      type: "number", answer: 78.5, tol: 0.3, unit: "cm²",
      hints: ["The base is a circle: A = r²·π.", "25 · 3.14 = ?"],
      why: "The cylinder <b>inherits</b> the prism idea: constant cross-section, so V = G·h – G just happens to be a circle." },
    { q: "The same can: volume (cm³)?",
      type: "number", answer: 785, tol: 3, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["V = G · h.", "78.5 · 10 = ?"],
      why: "V = r²π·h – <b>three topics chained</b>: circle, prism, power. Your web holds." },
    { q: "1 litre = 1000 cm³. Does the can (785 cm³) hold more or less than a litre?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["Less – 785 cm³ < 1000 cm³", "More", "Exactly one litre", "Impossible to know"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Compare 785 with 1000."],
      why: "Volume ↔ litres connects geometry with the <b>supermarket shelf</b>. (A typical drink can: 500 cm³ = 0.5 L.)" },
    { q: "Sphere volume grows with r³. An orange with r = 8 cm instead of r = 4 cm: how many times more fruit?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["8 times", "2 times", "4 times", "16 times"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["It's r³ that counts.", "8³ : 4³ = 512 : 64 = ?"],
      why: "Lengths ×2 → volume ×8. The <b>k³ effect</b>: why big fruit feels so generous – and why mice freeze faster than elephants." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "wahrsch", title: "Probability", klasse: 8, area: "daten",
  x: -135, y: -1915, requires: ["statistik"],
  teaser: "Six correct lottery numbers: 1 in 8 million. Being struck by lightning: more likely. Why does everyone play anyway?",
  desc: "Computing chance – mathematics' youngest superpower.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A die: what is the probability of rolling a 6?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["1/6", "1/2", "1/3", "6 %"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["6 equally likely faces.", "Exactly one of them wins."],
      why: "P = <b>favourable cases / possible cases</b> – chance becomes measurable. (Hello, fractions!)" },
    { q: "In a bag: 3 red, 2 blue and 5 green marbles. P(red) as a percentage?",
      type: "number", answer: 30, unit: "%",
      hints: ["10 marbles in total, 3 favourable.", "3/10 – and that in percent?"],
      why: "Probability, fraction, percent: <b>the same idea in three outfits</b>." },
    { q: "A fair coin shows heads 5 times in a row. What holds for the 6th toss?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["Again exactly 50:50 – the coin has no memory", "Tails is now overdue and more likely", "Heads is on a streak and more likely", "The 6th toss can be computed: 1/64 for heads"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Can the coin remember the previous tosses?", "Every toss starts from zero."],
      why: "The 'balancing-out belief' is THE classic error about chance (gambler's fallacy) – casinos live off it." },
    { q: "A wheel of fortune has 8 equal sectors, 2 of them win. You spin 100 times. Roughly how often do you win?",
      type: "number", answer: 25,
      hints: ["P(win) = 2/8 = 1/4.", "A quarter of 100."],
      why: "<b>Expectation</b> = P · number of tries. That's how insurers, casinos – and smart players – calculate." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "pyramide", title: "Pyramids & Cones", klasse: 8, area: "geometrie",
  x: 66, y: 1918, requires: ["prisma"],
  teaser: "Fill a pyramid with water and pour it into a prism with the same base and height: it fits exactly 3 times!",
  desc: "Pointed solids – and the mysterious factor 1/3.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "The water experiment shows: pyramid = 1/3 of the matching prism. So V(pyramid) = ?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["G · h : 3", "G · h", "G · h : 2", "G + h"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["The prism holds G · h.", "The pyramid fits into it exactly 3 times."],
      why: "<b>V = G·h/3</b> – discovered by experiment, provable with calculus later. The 1/3 is no accident!" },
    { q: "A square pyramid: base side 6 cm, height 10 cm. Volume (cm³)?",
      type: "number", answer: 120, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["Base area G = 6 · 6 = 36.", "V = 36 · 10 : 3."],
      why: "Base × height ÷ 3 – the recipe for <b>every</b> pointed solid." },
    { q: "A cone is a 'round pyramid': r = 3 cm (G = r²π ≈ 28.26), h = 10 cm. V ≈ ? (cm³, π ≈ 3.14)",
      type: "number", answer: 94.2, tol: 0.5, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["Same formula: G · h : 3.", "28.26 · 10 : 3 = ?"],
      why: "Prism→cylinder, pyramid→cone: round versions <b>inherit</b> the formulas. The web builds on itself." },
    { q: "The Great Pyramid: base ≈ 230 m × 230 m, height ≈ 146 m. Roughly its volume?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["≈ 2.6 million m³", "≈ 7.7 million m³ (that would be the prism!)", "≈ 33 600 m³", "≈ 230 000 m³"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["G = 230² = 52 900 m².", "52 900 · 146 ≈ 7.7 million – and now the ÷3."],
      why: "2.6 million m³ of stone, placed 4500 years ago – and you just <b>computed</b> it from three numbers." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "kugel", title: "The Sphere", klasse: 8, area: "geometrie",
  x: -214, y: 2039, requires: ["zylinder"],
  teaser: "Archimedes was so proud of this discovery that he had it carved on his tombstone.",
  desc: "The perfect solid: every point equally far from the centre.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A sphere fits snugly in a cylinder (same r, height 2r). Archimedes proved: the sphere fills …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["exactly 2/3 of the cylinder – his tombstone engraving!", "exactly 1/2", "exactly 3/4", "exactly π/4"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Cylinder: V = r²π · 2r = 2r³π. Sphere: V = (4/3)r³π.", "(4/3) : 2 = ?"],
      why: "V(sphere) = <b>(4/3) r³ π</b> – and the elegant 2/3 ratio was Archimedes' favourite result of all." },
    { q: "A ball with r = 3 cm: V = (4/3)·r³·π ≈ ? (cm³, π ≈ 3.14)",
      type: "number", answer: 113.04, tol: 0.6, unit: "cm³",
      hints: ["r³ = 27.", "(4/3) · 27 = 36, then · 3.14."],
      why: "From marbles to planets: <b>one formula</b> for every sphere in the universe." },
    { q: "Surface: A = 4r²π – exactly 4 'great circles'! For r = 5 cm (π ≈ 3.14): A ≈ ? (cm²)",
      type: "number", answer: 314, tol: 1, unit: "cm²",
      hints: ["r² = 25, so one great circle: 25π ≈ 78.5.", "Times 4."],
      why: "Peel an orange and lay the peel into circles of its size: it fills <b>four</b>. Test it in the kitchen!" },
    { q: "Why are soap bubbles spheres?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["The sphere encloses the most volume with the least surface", "Air presses them round from inside only", "Because they spin", "Coincidence"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["The soap skin wants to be as small as possible.", "Which shape wraps a given volume with minimal skin?"],
      why: "Nature <b>optimizes</b> – and the sphere is the optimum. Math predicts the shape of bubbles, drops and planets." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "faktorisieren", title: "Factoring", klasse: 8, area: "algebra",
  x: 1233, y: -1471, requires: ["termeb"],
  teaser: "Multiplying out is easy – the real detective work is running it backwards.",
  desc: "Products out of sums: the reverse gear of expanding.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "6x + 9 = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3(2x + 3)", "6(x + 9)", "3(2x + 9)", "9(6x + 1)"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Which number divides both 6x and 9?", "Pull the 3 out in front: 6x = 3·2x and 9 = 3·3."],
      why: "<b>Factoring out</b> = the distributive law backwards. Check by expanding!" },
    { q: "x² − 25 = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(x − 5)(x + 5)", "(x − 5)²", "x(x − 25)", "can't be factored"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["A difference of two squares: x² and 5².", "Check: (a−b)(a+b) = a² − b² – the middle terms cancel."],
      why: "The <b>third binomial formula</b> backwards: a² − b² = (a−b)(a+b). A pattern you'll meet a hundred times." },
    { q: "Mental math: 51 · 49 = (50+1)(50−1) = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 2499,
      hints: ["(a+b)(a−b) = a² − b².", "2500 − 1 = ?"],
      why: "Patterns make you <b>faster than a calculator</b> – if you see the structure." },
    { q: "Why factor at all? x² − 25 = 0 becomes (x−5)(x+5) = 0. Therefore …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["x = 5 or x = −5: a product is 0 only if a factor is 0", "x = 25", "x = ±25", "no solution exists"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["When is a · b = 0?", "Only if a = 0 or b = 0. Apply that to the two brackets."],
      why: "The <b>zero-product property</b> – the key that unlocks quadratic equations. Next stop: exactly there." }
  ]
},
{
