      why: "<b>Personification</b> gives human traits to non-human things." }
  ]
},

/* ================= GRADE 10 ================= */
{
  id: "quadgl", title: "Quadratic Equations", klasse: 10, area: "algebra",
  x: 2078, y: -1200, requires: ["faktorisieren", "wurzeln"],
  teaser: "x² = 49 has TWO answers. Double the solutions, double the fun.",
  desc: "Equations with x² – and the art of catching both solutions.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "x² = 49. Solutions?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["x = 7 or x = −7", "only x = 7", "x = 24.5", "x = ±24.5"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Which numbers squared give 49?", "Don't forget: (−7)² = 49 too!"],
      why: "Squaring swallows the sign – so the reverse spits out <b>two</b> candidates: ±√." },
    { q: "x² − 6x = 0  →  x(x − 6) = 0. Besides 0, x = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 6,
      hints: ["Factor out x, then zero-product property.", "The second factor: x − 6 = 0."],
      why: "<b>Factoring</b> cracks quadratics: product = 0 → one factor = 0. Your grade-8 tool pays off." },
    { q: "x² − 5x + 6 = 0. Find two numbers with sum 5 and product 6:",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(x−2)(x−3) = 0 → x = 2 or x = 3", "x = 1 or x = 6", "x = −2 or x = −3", "no solution"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Which pairs multiply to 6? 1·6, 2·3 …", "Which pair also adds to 5?"],
      why: "Sum & product: quadratics often factor <b>by looking</b> – before any formula." },
    { q: "A ball's height: h = 20t − 5t². When does it land (h = 0, t > 0)?",
      type: "number", answer: 4, unit: "s",
      hints: ["Factor: t(20 − 5t) = 0.", "t = 0 is the launch. The other factor: 20 − 5t = 0."],
      why: "Thrown objects obey <b>quadratic laws</b> – next stop: the parabola they fly along." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "quadfunk", title: "Parabolas", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: 1543, y: -1838, requires: ["linfunk", "quadgl"],
  teaser: "Basketball shots, water fountains, satellite dishes: one curve rules them all.",
  desc: "Quadratic functions: when growth itself grows.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "y = x²: from x = 2 to x = 4, y jumps from 4 to 16. The graph is …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["a curve getting ever steeper – a parabola", "a straight line", "a circle", "flat"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Straight lines grow by the same amount per step.", "Here the steps grow: +5, +7, +9, … (from 4→9→16→25)."],
      why: "x² grows <b>faster and faster</b> – its graph bends. Lines are out; welcome, curves!" },
    { q: "y = (x − 3)² + 2 has its lowest point (vertex) at …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(3|2)", "(−3|2)", "(3|−2)", "(0|2)"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["A square is never negative – at best 0.", "(x−3)² = 0 exactly when x = 3. Then y = 2."],
      why: "<b>Vertex form</b>: the formula openly displays its lowest point. Read the shift, don't compute it." },
    { q: "Where does y = x² − 4x + 3 cross the x-axis? Solve x² − 4x + 3 = 0: x = 1 and x = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 3,
      hints: ["Two numbers: sum 4, product 3.", "1 · 3 = 3 and 1 + 3 = 4."],
      why: "Zeros of the function = solutions of the equation – <b>functions and equations are one world</b>." },
    { q: "Why are satellite dishes parabolic?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["All incoming parallel rays reflect into ONE focus point", "It looks futuristic", "Cheapest shape to build", "Less wind resistance"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Where does the receiver sit? At a very special point.", "The parabola bundles parallel signals into it."],
      why: "The parabola's <b>focus property</b> – also in headlights (reversed) and solar cookers. Geometry becomes technology." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "folgen", title: "Sequences & Limits", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: 335, y: -2376, requires: ["linfunk", "potenzen"],
  teaser: "1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, … The terms never reach 0 – yet 0 is exactly where they're heading.",
  desc: "Number chains with direction – and the idea that tames infinity.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Arithmetic sequence: 5, 8, 11, 14, … (always +3). What is term number 10?",
      type: "number", answer: 32,
      hints: ["From term 1 to term 10 there are 9 steps.", "5 + 9 · 3 = ?"],
      why: "aₙ = a₁ + (n−1)·d – the <b>linear function</b> in disguise, walking in steps." },
    { q: "Geometric sequence: 3, 6, 12, 24, … (always ×2). Term number 6?",
      type: "number", answer: 96,
      hints: ["From term 1 five doublings happen.", "3 · 2⁵ = ?"],
      why: "aₙ = a₁ · qⁿ⁻¹ – <b>powers</b> in disguise. Two sequence types, two old friends." },
    { q: "1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, … The limit of this sequence is …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["0 – the terms get arbitrarily close, without ever arriving", "1/32", "it has none", "−∞"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Where are the terms heading?", "Name ANY tiny distance from 0 – eventually all terms are closer than that."],
      why: "A <b>limit</b> is a destination, not a member of the journey. This idea unlocks all of calculus." },
    { q: "0.999… (nines forever) = ?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["exactly 1", "slightly less than 1", "0.999", "undefined"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["1/3 = 0.333… Multiply both sides by 3.", "3 · 1/3 = 1 and 3 · 0.333… = 0.999…"],
      why: "Infinite processes have exact values – <b>limits make infinity computable</b>. Mind officially blown." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "expfunk", title: "Exponential & Logarithm", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: 1090, y: -2138, requires: ["potrat", "folgen"],
  teaser: "Growth that multiplies beats growth that adds – always, eventually. Ask any virus or bank account.",
  desc: "The function of explosions and decays – and its inverse, the logarithm.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "Bacteria double every hour, starting at 100. After 5 hours?",
      type: "number", answer: 3200,
      hints: ["100 · 2⁵.", "100 · 32 = ?"],
      why: "y = 100 · 2ˣ – the variable sits <b>in the exponent</b>: that's what 'exponential' means." },
    { q: "Linear +50/h versus exponential ×2/h, both start at 100. After 10 hours?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["Exponential wins massively: 102 400 vs 600", "Linear wins", "A tie", "Depends on luck"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Linear: 100 + 10·50.", "Exponential: 100 · 2¹⁰ = 100 · 1024."],
      why: "<b>Multiplying beats adding</b> – eventually by absurd margins. Pandemics, compound interest, viral videos." },
    { q: "The logarithm answers which question?",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["'2 to WHAT power gives 1024?' → log₂(1024) = 10", "'What is 2 · 1024?'", "'What is 1024 squared?'", "'Is 1024 prime?'"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Powers: base and exponent give the result.", "The log finds the missing EXPONENT."],
      why: "log is the <b>reverse gear of the exponential</b> – like root for squares. Every operation gets its undo." },
    { q: "Half-life 10 years; 80 g of a substance today. After 30 years (g)?",
      type: "number", answer: 10, unit: "g",
      hints: ["30 years = 3 halvings.", "80 → 40 → 20 → ?"],
      why: "Exponential <b>decay</b>: same law, factor ½. Radiocarbon dating reads ages straight from it." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "sinfunk", title: "Sine Waves", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: -899, y: 2225, requires: ["trig"],
  teaser: "Spin a point on a circle and record its height over time: you just drew sound, light and tides.",
  desc: "From triangle ratio to the curve of everything that swings.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A point circles at constant speed; we plot its height against time. The curve …",
      type: "choice",
      choices: ["swings smoothly up and down – the sine wave", "is a straight line", "is a parabola", "jumps abruptly"],
      answer: 0,
      hints: ["Height climbs, peaks at the top, sinks, bottoms out …", "… and then everything repeats."],
      why: "sin leaves the triangle: as a <b>function of the angle</b> it describes anything that turns or swings." },
    { q: "After which angle does the sine wave repeat?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["360° – one full circle", "180°", "90°", "never"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["When is the circling point back at its start?"],
      why: "<b>Periodicity</b>: sin(x + 360°) = sin(x). Repetition is the wave's superpower." },
    { q: "The largest value sine ever reaches?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["1 (at 90°)", "0", "∞", "360"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Height of the circling point at the very top.", "The circle has radius 1."],
      why: "<b>Amplitude</b>: sine lives between −1 and 1. Loudness of sound = amplitude of its wave." },
    { q: "sin(30°) = ? (as a decimal)",
      type: "number", answer: 0.5,
      hints: ["You know this from the ramp!", "Half."],
      why: "Same value, new meaning: the triangle ratio became a <b>point on the wave</b>. Music, AC power, radio – all sine." }
  ]
},

{
  id: "polynome", title: "Polynomials & Their Roots", klasse: 10, area: "algebra",
  x: 1137, y: -2393, requires: ["quadgl","potenzen"],
  teaser: "Beyond parabolas: curves that wiggle. How many times can they cross zero?",
  desc: "Higher-degree polynomials, factoring, and counting roots.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "A polynomial of degree 3 can have at most how many real roots?",
      type: "choice", choices: ["3","1","2","infinitely many"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Degree = highest power.","Roots <= degree."],
      why: "A degree-n polynomial has <b>at most n</b> real roots — degree caps the crossings." },
    { q: "Solve x³ − x = 0 by factoring: x(x−1)(x+1)=0. How many different roots?",
      type: "number", answer: 3,
      hints: ["Set each factor to 0.","x = 0, 1, −1."],
      why: "<b>Factoring</b> turns 'find the roots' into 'read them off'." },
    { q: "The graph of an odd-degree polynomial such as x³ …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["falls on one end and rises on the other","is a closed loop","never crosses zero","is a straight line"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Think of the shape of x³.","Opposite ends go opposite ways."],
      why: "<b>Odd degree</b> → opposite end behaviour, so it must cross zero at least once." },
    { q: "How many DISTINCT roots does (x−2)²(x+5) have?",
      type: "number", answer: 2,
      hints: ["x = 2 (twice) and x = −5.","Count different values."],
      why: "x=2 is a <b>double root</b>; distinct roots are 2 and −5." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "gebrochen", title: "Rational Functions & Asymptotes", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: 1269, y: -1930, requires: ["quadfunk","potrat"],
  teaser: "Divide by x and watch the graph flee to infinity — chasing a line it never touches.",
  desc: "Fractions of polynomials: poles, gaps and asymptotes.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "f(x) = 1/x has a vertical asymptote at …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["x = 0","x = 1","y = 1","nowhere"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Where does the denominator become 0?","1/0 is undefined."],
      why: "A <b>pole</b> appears where the denominator hits zero — the graph shoots to infinity." },
    { q: "As x grows huge, the value of 1/x approaches …",
      type: "number", answer: 0,
      hints: ["1 divided by a bigger and bigger number.","It shrinks toward nothing."],
      why: "y = 0 is a <b>horizontal asymptote</b> — approached forever, never reached." },
    { q: "A removable 'hole' in the graph appears when a factor cancels in …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["both numerator and denominator","only the numerator","only the denominator","neither"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["It cancels top and bottom.","Undefined, but fillable."],
      why: "A shared factor makes a <b>hole</b>; a denominator-only zero makes a pole." },
    { q: "For f(x) = 1/(x − 3), the vertical asymptote is at x = ?",
      type: "number", answer: 3,
      hints: ["Denominator zero.","x − 3 = 0."],
      why: "The graph blows up where the <b>denominator hits zero</b>." }
  ]
},
{
  id: "einheitskreis", title: "Unit Circle & Radians", klasse: 10, area: "analysis",
  x: 1679, y: -2050, requires: ["sinfunk"],
  teaser: "Wrap the number line around a circle of radius 1 — and sine and cosine become mere coordinates.",
  desc: "Angles as arc length: radians and the unit-circle definition of sine and cosine.",
  exercises: [
    { q: "On the unit circle, cos θ is the … of the point.",
      type: "choice", choices: ["x-coordinate","y-coordinate","radius","arc length"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Cosine = horizontal, sine = vertical.","x for cos."],
      why: "On the <b>unit circle</b>, (cos θ, sin θ) IS the point — trigonometry becomes geometry." },
    { q: "A full turn around the circle is 2π radians ≈ ? (2 decimals)",
      type: "number", answer: 6.28, tol: 0.02,
      hints: ["2 · π, and π ≈ 3.14159.","2 · 3.14159…"],
      why: "<b>Radians</b> measure angle as arc length — the natural unit for calculus." },
    { q: "Half a turn around the circle is π radians ≈ ? (2 decimals)",
      type: "number", answer: 3.14, tol: 0.01,
      hints: ["π ≈ 3.14159.","Just π."],
      why: "<b>π radians = 180°</b> — the half-turn." },
    { q: "At angle 0 on the unit circle, the point (cos, sin) is …",
      type: "choice", choices: ["(1, 0)","(0, 1)","(0, 0)","(1, 1)"], answer: 0,
      hints: ["Start on the positive x-axis.","cos 0 = 1, sin 0 = 0."],
      why: "Angle 0 sits at <b>(1, 0)</b> — the starting gate of the circle." }
  ]
},

